From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:46:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] accel/tcg: rr vCPU loop takes the replay mutex if replay
 started under it

The round-robin vCPU loop does

    bql_unlock(); replay_mutex_lock(); bql_lock();
    ... icount accounting and timers ...
    replay_mutex_unlock();

With replay off, the lock is a no-op. If the vCPU thread is waiting in
bql_lock() at that point while the main loop runs x-rr-begin-record
(0039), replay_enable() switches to RECORD and hands the mutex to the
main thread (replay_mutex_init()). The vCPU then gets the BQL, runs the
icount part without the mutex, and calls replay_mutex_unlock() with
replay on and the mutex not held:

    replay_mutex_unlock: assertion failed: (replay_mutex_locked())

A stop and cont (or a loadvm and vm_start just before the begin, as
PANDA's begin_record with a snapshot does) kicks the vCPU out of
rr_wait_io_event() into exactly that window, so it shows up as an
intermittent abort at the start of a recording.

replay_mode only changes under the BQL, so once the vCPU has the BQL the
mode is stable: if replay is on and this thread does not hold the
mutex, drop the BQL and take both again in lock order. The VM is
stopped across the switch, so no guest event is lost or reordered.
Costs one well-predicted branch per loop iteration when replay is off.
---
 accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-rr.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-rr.c b/accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-rr.c
index cdaa3e118082d7f100dd7655e57e28b55418d294..94755a2cfb70fa000552dc5dd42ff401a0c28f65 100644
--- a/accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-rr.c
+++ b/accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-rr.c
@@ -239,6 +239,19 @@ static void *rr_cpu_thread_fn(void *arg)
         bql_unlock();
         replay_mutex_lock();
         bql_lock();
+        /*
+         * x-rr-begin-record and x-rr-begin-replay turn record/replay on
+         * from the main loop, under the BQL.  If that happened while we
+         * waited for the BQL, the lock above was a no-op (replay was off)
+         * and we hold the BQL without the replay mutex: take it now, in
+         * lock order.  replay_mode only changes under the BQL, so it is
+         * stable here.
+         */
+        while (replay_mode != REPLAY_MODE_NONE && !replay_mutex_locked()) {
+            bql_unlock();
+            replay_mutex_lock();
+            bql_lock();
+        }
 
         if (icount_enabled()) {
             int cpu_count = rr_cpu_count();
